// ALG1 Unit 04 Lesson 05 Quiz
// Lesson: Interpreting Function Behavior
// MLA Standards: MLA.A1.AR.8; MLA.A1.FN.3

::ALG1_U04_L05_Q01::[Question ID: ALG1_U04_L05_Q01] [MLA Standard: MLA.A1.FN.3]
A function has \(f(0)=12\). Which feature is shown?
{
=y-intercept#Correct. The y-intercept is the output when the input is 0.
~x-intercept#An x-intercept occurs when the output is 0, not when the input is 0.
~zero#A zero is an input that gives output 0. Here the output is 12.
~minimum#The value at x = 0 is not automatically the lowest output.
}

::ALG1_U04_L05_Q02::[Question ID: ALG1_U04_L05_Q02] [MLA Standard: MLA.A1.FN.3]
A graph has an x-intercept at \((8,0)\). What is the zero of the function?
{
~0#The output is 0 at the intercept, but the zero is the input value.
~4#This value is not shown by the intercept.
=8#Correct. The zero is the x-value where the output equals 0.
~(0,8)#This reverses the coordinates of the x-intercept.
}

::ALG1_U04_L05_Q03::[Question ID: ALG1_U04_L05_Q03] [MLA Standard: MLA.A1.FN.3]
A function increases from \(x=-2\) to \(x=3\). What does that mean?
{
~The outputs get smaller as x moves from -2 to 3.#That describes decreasing, not increasing.
=The outputs get larger as x moves from -2 to 3.#Correct. Increasing means outputs rise as inputs move left to right.
~The output is always 0.#Increasing does not mean the function stays at 0.
~The graph has no intercepts.#Increasing behavior does not determine whether intercepts exist.
}

::ALG1_U04_L05_Q04::[Question ID: ALG1_U04_L05_Q04] [MLA Standard: MLA.A1.FN.3]
A function decreases from \(x=1\) to \(x=7\). Which statement is true?
{
~The outputs rise as x increases.#That describes increasing behavior.
~The output is 0 for every x-value.#A decreasing function changes, so it is not always 0.
~The y-intercept must be 7.#The interval endpoint does not determine the y-intercept.
=The outputs fall as x increases from 1 to 7.#Correct. Decreasing means outputs go down as inputs increase.
}

::ALG1_U04_L05_Q05::[Question ID: ALG1_U04_L05_Q05] [MLA Standard: MLA.A1.AR.8]
What is the vertex of \(y=|x-2|\)?
{
~(0, 2)#This is the y-intercept, not the vertex.
=(2, 0)#Correct. The absolute value is 0 when x = 2, so the vertex is (2, 0).
~(-2, 0)#The expression \(x-2\) is zero when x = 2, not -2.
~(2, 2)#At x = 2, the output is 0, not 2.
}

::ALG1_U04_L05_Q06::[Question ID: ALG1_U04_L05_Q06] [MLA Standard: MLA.A1.AR.8]
For \(y=|x+3|\), what is the minimum output value?
{
=0#Correct. Absolute value cannot be negative, and it reaches 0 when x = -3.
~-3#The center input is -3, but the output value is 0.
~3#This is the distance from 0 to -3, not the minimum output of the function.
~6#This can be an output for some inputs, but it is not the minimum.
}

::ALG1_U04_L05_Q07::[Question ID: ALG1_U04_L05_Q07] [MLA Standard: MLA.A1.FN.3]
A ball reaches its highest point at 5 seconds. Which feature is being described?
{
~zero#A zero occurs when the height is 0.
~y-intercept#The y-intercept is the starting height at time 0.
=maximum#Correct. The highest point is the maximum output.
~domain#The domain is the set of possible input values.
}

::ALG1_U04_L05_Q08::[Question ID: ALG1_U04_L05_Q08] [MLA Standard: MLA.A1.FN.3]
A tank is empty after 10 minutes. If \(V(t)\) gives volume, what feature does \(t=10\) represent?
{
~maximum#Empty means the output volume is 0, not the largest value.
~y-intercept#The y-intercept occurs at t = 0.
~rate of change#The time when volume is 0 is a zero, not the rate.
=zero#Correct. \(V(10)=0\), so t = 10 is a zero of the function.
}

::ALG1_U04_L05_Q09::[Question ID: ALG1_U04_L05_Q09] [MLA Standard: MLA.A1.FN.3]
If a graph changes from decreasing to increasing at \((4,2)\), what feature is \((4,2)\)?
{
=minimum#Correct. A change from decreasing to increasing gives a lowest point.
~maximum#A maximum occurs when a graph changes from increasing to decreasing.
~x-intercept#An x-intercept has y = 0, but this point has y = 2.
~zero#A zero is an input where the output is 0.
}

::ALG1_U04_L05_Q10::[Question ID: ALG1_U04_L05_Q10] [MLA Standard: MLA.A1.FN.3]
The y-intercept of a savings function is 40. What does this mean?
{
~The account loses $40 each month.#Loss over time is rate of change, not the starting output.
=The account starts with $40.#Correct. The y-intercept is the output when the input is 0.
~The account reaches $0 after 40 months.#That describes a zero, not a y-intercept.
~The maximum savings is $40.#The starting value is not necessarily the greatest value.
}

::ALG1_U04_L05_Q11::[Question ID: ALG1_U04_L05_Q11] [MLA Standard: MLA.A1.AR.8]
Which input makes \(y=|x-7|\) equal to 0?
{
~0#Substituting 0 gives \(|-7|=7\), not 0.
~1#Substituting 1 gives \(|-6|=6\), not 0.
=7#Correct. \(|x-7|=0\) when x = 7.
~-7#Substituting -7 gives \(|-14|=14\), not 0.
}

::ALG1_U04_L05_Q12::[Question ID: ALG1_U04_L05_Q12] [MLA Standard: MLA.A1.FN.3]
A function has a maximum output of 30 at input 6. Which statement is true?
{
~The output can never be 30.#The maximum output is exactly 30.
~The input can never be 6.#The maximum occurs at input 6.
~The graph must cross the x-axis at 30.#A maximum output is not an x-intercept.
=The greatest output shown is 30.#Correct. A maximum is the greatest output value on the interval or graph shown.
}

::ALG1_U04_L05_Q13::[Question ID: ALG1_U04_L05_Q13] [MLA Standard: MLA.A1.FN.3]
A function has a zero at \(x=-4\). Which ordered pair must be on the graph?
{
=(-4, 0)#Correct. A zero is an input where the output is 0.
~(0, -4)#This is a y-intercept form, not a zero at x = -4.
~(-4, -4)#The output must be 0 at a zero.
~(4, 0)#This would show a zero at x = 4, not x = -4.
}

::ALG1_U04_L05_Q14::[Question ID: ALG1_U04_L05_Q14] [MLA Standard: MLA.A1.FN.3]
A graph is flat from \(x=2\) to \(x=5\). How is the function behaving on that interval?
{
~increasing#A flat graph does not rise.
~decreasing#A flat graph does not fall.
~undefined#A flat graph still has defined output values.
=constant#Correct. A flat section means the output stays the same.
}

::ALG1_U04_L05_Q15::[Question ID: ALG1_U04_L05_Q15] [MLA Standard: MLA.A1.AR.8]
What does \(y=|x-10|\) measure?
{
~The product of x and 10#Absolute value is not multiplication.
=The distance between x and 10#Correct. \(|x-10|\) gives how far x is from 10.
~The sum of x and 10#The expression subtracts inside absolute value.
~Only values greater than 10#Inputs less than 10 also have distances from 10.
}

::ALG1_U04_L05_Q16::[Question ID: ALG1_U04_L05_Q16] [MLA Standard: MLA.A1.FN.3]
Which feature tells where a graph crosses the y-axis?
{
~zero#A zero tells where the output is 0.
~x-intercept#An x-intercept is where the graph crosses the x-axis.
=y-intercept#Correct. The y-intercept is where the graph crosses the y-axis.
~domain#The domain is the set of inputs.
}

::ALG1_U04_L05_Q17::[Question ID: ALG1_U04_L05_Q17] [MLA Standard: MLA.A1.FN.3]
A candle's height decreases over time. What does the decreasing behavior mean?
{
=The candle gets shorter as time passes.#Correct. Decreasing means the output height goes down as the input time increases.
~The candle gets taller as time passes.#That would be increasing behavior.
~The candle height stays the same.#That would be constant behavior.
~The candle begins at 0 height.#Starting height is the y-intercept, not decreasing behavior.
}

::ALG1_U04_L05_Q18::[Question ID: ALG1_U04_L05_Q18] [MLA Standard: MLA.A1.FN.3]
A function has y-intercept \((0,-3)\). What is the output when the input is 0?
{
~-6#This is not shown by the y-intercept.
=-3#Correct. The y-intercept \((0,-3)\) means \(f(0)=-3\).
~0#Zero is the input coordinate, not the output coordinate.
~3#The output shown is negative 3, not positive 3.
}

::ALG1_U04_L05_Q19::[Question ID: ALG1_U04_L05_Q19] [MLA Standard: MLA.A1.AR.8]
Which statement is true about \(y=|x|\)?
{
~The graph has outputs that are always negative.#Absolute value outputs are never negative.
~The graph has no minimum.#The graph has a minimum at the origin.
=The minimum value is 0.#Correct. The smallest possible value of \(|x|\) is 0.
~The graph is a horizontal line.#The graph is V-shaped, not horizontal.
}

::ALG1_U04_L05_Q20::[Question ID: ALG1_U04_L05_Q20] [MLA Standard: MLA.A1.FN.3]
A car's distance from home increases from 0 to 2 hours, then stays the same from 2 to 3 hours. What does the flat part mean?
{
~The car returns home.#Returning home would make distance decrease.
~The car moves farther from home.#Moving farther would make distance increase.
~The car is below the x-axis.#Distance is not described as negative here.
=The car's distance from home does not change.#Correct. A flat part means the output stays constant.
}

::ALG1_U04_L05_Q21::[Question ID: ALG1_U04_L05_Q21] [MLA Standard: MLA.A1.FN.3]
What feature is represented by the point where a graph touches the x-axis?
{
=x-intercept#Correct. A point on the x-axis has output 0, so it is an x-intercept.
~y-intercept#A y-intercept is where the graph crosses the y-axis.
~maximum only#A graph can touch the x-axis without being a maximum.
~range#The range is the set of output values, not one crossing point.
}

::ALG1_U04_L05_Q22::[Question ID: ALG1_U04_L05_Q22] [MLA Standard: MLA.A1.AR.8]
For \(y=|x+4|\), where is the vertex?
{
~(4, 0)#The expression \(x+4\) is zero when x = -4.
=(-4, 0)#Correct. \(|x+4|=0\) at x = -4, so the vertex is (-4, 0).
~(0, 4)#This is the y-intercept, not the vertex.
~(0, -4)#This point is not on the graph because absolute value outputs are not negative.
}

::ALG1_U04_L05_Q23::[Question ID: ALG1_U04_L05_Q23] [MLA Standard: MLA.A1.FN.3]
If \(f(3)=0\), what can you conclude?
{
~The y-intercept is 3.#The y-intercept is the output when input is 0.
~The maximum is 3.#The statement does not describe a highest output.
=The function has a zero at x = 3.#Correct. A zero is an input that produces output 0.
~The range is only 0.#One output value does not determine the entire range.
}

::ALG1_U04_L05_Q24::[Question ID: ALG1_U04_L05_Q24] [MLA Standard: MLA.A1.FN.3]
A water level reaches its lowest point at 2 inches. Which feature is described?
{
~maximum#A maximum is the highest output.
~x-intercept#An x-intercept occurs when output is 0.
~y-intercept#A y-intercept occurs when the input is 0.
=minimum#Correct. The lowest output is the minimum.
}

::ALG1_U04_L05_Q25::[Question ID: ALG1_U04_L05_Q25] [MLA Standard: MLA.A1.FN.3]
Which interpretation matches a zero of a profit function?
{
=The business breaks even.#Correct. Profit is 0 at a break-even point.
~The business has its greatest profit.#Greatest profit would be a maximum, not a zero.
~The starting profit is shown.#A starting value is the y-intercept.
~The profit is increasing forever.#A zero does not describe long-term increasing behavior.
}
